The code statements that lead to the bug are:
ap->deferred_qc = NULL;
queue_work(system_highpri_wq, &ap->deferred_qc_work);

Reason:
When `ap->deferred_qc` is cleared (e.g., due to a timeout or error) without canceling the already scheduled work item, the work can still execute later after new commands have been queued. The work function (`ata_scsi_deferred_qc_work`) will then attempt to issue a stale or wrong command, and the check `ap->ops->qc_defer()` may return non‑zero (because new NCQ commands are in flight), triggering the WARN_ON.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
cancel_work(&ap->deferred_qc_work);

Reason:
Adding `cancel_work(&ap->deferred_qc_work)` immediately after clearing `ap->deferred_qc` ensures that any previously scheduled work is removed, preventing it from running at an incorrect time. This eliminates the race condition where the work would execute after the deferred command has been replaced or while new commands are active.

